      "message": "hi, we are already calling the same `scan::detail::parseProbeCommand` in line 35.\n\nFound only one other caller in `src/entity_manager/perform_scan.cpp +729` which can also access `struct EMConfig`\n\n```\n  void scan::PerformScan::processConfigurations(\n      std::flat_set\u003cstd::string, std::less\u003c\u003e\u003e\u0026 dbusProbeInterfaces,\n      std::vector\u003cstd::shared_ptr\u003cprobe::PerformProbe\u003e\u003e\u0026 dbusProbePointers)\n  {\n      for (auto it \u003d _configurations.begin(); it !\u003d _configurations.end();)\n      {   \n          if (std::find(passedProbes.begin(), passedProbes.end(), it-\u003ename) !\u003d\n              passedProbes.end())\n          {   \n              it \u003d _configurations.erase(it);\n              continue;\n          }   \n  \n          EMConfig\u0026 recordRef \u003d *it;\n          std::vector\u003cprobe::Token\u003e probeCommand \u003d\n              detail::parseProbeCommand(it-\u003eprobeStmt);\n          if (probeCommand.empty())\n          {\n              lg2::error(\"Skipping configuration {NAME}: invalid Probe statement\",\n                         \"NAME\", it-\u003ename);\n              it++;\n              continue;\n          }\n  \n```\n\nwould it make sense to store the `std::vector\u003cprobe::Token\u003e` in `struct EMConfig` to avoid parsing it 3 times ?\n\nI don\u0027t think the probes change at runtime so it should be fine to store the output and use it instead of parsing again.\n\nThe `EMConfig` constructor could call the `parseProbeCommand` function, perform the check and fill the member, guaranteeing each instance is valid.\n\nWould it work for you like this ?",
